    <Program class="ca.nrcan.gc.OEE.ERS.ErsProgram">
        <Labels>
            <English>EnerGuide Rating System</English>
            <French>Syst??????me de cote ??????nerGuide</French>
        </Labels>
        <Version major="15" minor="7" build="19126">
            <Labels>
                <English>v15.7b19126</English>
                <French>v15.7b19126</French>
            </Labels>
        </Version>
        <SdkVersion major="1" minor="17" build="19126">
            <Labels>
                <English>v1.17b19126</English>
                <French>v1.17b19126</French>
            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="true" waterConservation="true" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <AtypicalElectricalLoads deIcingCables="false" exteriorLighting="false" hotTub="false" heatedGarage="true" portableAirConditioning="false" pool="false" mixedUse="false" totalUnits="0" energyStarUnits="0" evChargingStation="false" outdoorGasAppliances="false" commonSpace="false" elevator="false" fitnessRoom="false" snowmeltSystem="false" unheatedGarage="false" />
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="3" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="0" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>The infiltration test shows that the air seal in the house is problematic. In fact, we noticed leakage coming from the windows, doors and exhausting fans (bathroom, kitchen). This situation leads to heat loss and therefore increases the required energy to heat the house. Heat loss can result in problems of condensation and even to the formation of mould.

This situation also makes the house less comfortable and cold.

</airSeal>
                <windows>The infiltration test shows that the windows and doors units are very leaky. We recommend the replacement of the units by ENERGY STAR certified product suited for northern climate (ZONE  3). The same recommendation is also applicable for the doors.

During the inspection the owner mentioned his intention to remove some windows and doors. This could be a good start, but eventually all units must be replaced.

</windows>
                <HeatingSystem>During the inspection, the client mentioned his intention to remove the heating system by a more efficient system like electric. We recommend the replacement of the existing furnace by an electric furnace for the following reasons:

-	Electric furnace are 100% efficient

-	No combustion gas back draft danger

-	No need to verify the oil tank level and nor either the state of the oil tank.

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>The renewable energy is a good way to be more independent and reduce your environmental footprint. The solar panel can bring a great amount of energy especially during the spring and summer season. The energy production could reduce significantly the electrical or fossil fuel energy consumption of your home. </Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>After evaluation, we recommend the replacement of all windows and doors to be done prior to the replacement of the heating system. In fact, a new heating system in a drafty house will not be more efficient than the actual system. However, a sealed house means that the natural ventilation might not be enough and therefore the house might require the installation of a ventilation unit to ensure a proper ventilation in your home. The more the house is airtight the more the level of humidity arises and leads to condensation on the cold surfaces like windows and doors. An HRV (Heating Recovery Ventilation) unit can be a good solution to ensure the ventilation with the option of keeping the heat from the air to pre-heat the fresh intake air. </AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
